human and animal associations with their parasites. Initial discoveries were parasites in tissues of Egyptian mummies and has now expanded to include human and animal remains from around the world. Parasites have also been found in coproliths from latrines and cave habitations from the Middle Pleistocene and from dinosaurs living in the early Cretaceous. Vectors and their parasites have been found in ambers that are from the Eocene. The application of modern molecular methods has added greatly to identification of these parasites of the past and have allowed new interpretations of the various agents that people have carried with them as they have traveled around the globe.", "catalogBreadth": "", "catalogDistr": "", "catalogEnrollmentPriority": null, "catalogLang": "", "catalogForbiddenOverlaps": "", "catalogAttribute": "", "catalogWhenOffered": "Spring.", "catalogComments": "", "catalogPrereqCoreq": "Prerequisite: an introductory biology course and BIOMS 3310, BIOMS 4310, or permission of instructor.", "catalogPrereq": null, "catalogCoreq": null, "catalogFee": "", "catalogSatisfiesReq": "", "catalogPermission": "", "catalogCourseSubfield": "", "catalogOutcomes": null, "acadCareer": "UG", "acadGroup": "AG", "lastTermsOffered": null, "exploreCriteriaIds": null}, {"strm": 2783, "crseId": 366623, "crseOfferNbr": 1, "subject": "BIOMS", "catalogNbr": "4340", "titleShort": "Cellular Microbiology", "titleLong": "Cellular and Molecular Microbial Pathogenesis: The Host Pathogen Interplay", "crseAttrs": [], "crseAttrValueGroups": [], "enrollGroups": [{"classSections": [{"ssrComponent": "LEC", "ssrComponentLong": "Lecture", "section": "001", "classNbr": 16969, "meetings": [{"classMtgNbr": 1, "timeStart": "09:40AM", "timeEnd": "10:55AM", "startDt": "02/08/2021", "endDt": "05/14/2021", "instructors": [{"instrAssignSeq": 1, "netid": "bcv8", "firstName": "Brian", "middleName": "C.", "lastName": "VanderVen"}], "pattern": "TR", "facilityDescr": "Online Meeting", "bldgDescr": "Virtual", "facilityDescrshort": "Online", "meetingTopicDescription": ""}], "notes": [], "campus": "MAIN", "campusDescr": "Ithaca", "location": "ITH", "locationDescr": "Ithaca, NY (Main Campus)", "startDt": "02/08/2021", "endDt": "05/25/2021", "addConsent": "N", "addConsentDescr": "No Special Consent Required", "isComponentGraded": true, "crseAttrs": [], "instructionMode": "SD", "instrModeDescrshort": "DL-SD", "instrModeDescr": "Distance Learning-Synchronous", "topicDescription": "", "openStatus": "O", "exploreCriteriaIds": null, "materials": []}], "unitsMinimum": 3, "unitsMaximum": 3, "componentsOptional": [], "componentsRequired": ["LEC"], "gradingBasis": "OPT", "gradingBasisShort": "Stdnt Opt", "gradingBasisLong": "Letter or S/U grades", "simpleCombinations": [], "sessionCode": "1", "sessionBeginDt": "02/08/2021", "sessionEndDt": "05/25/2021", "sessionLong": "Regular Academic Session", "exploreCriteriaIds": null}], "description": "This course will integrate the immune response of the host to challenges from medically important pathogens, including viruses, parasites and bacteria, to provide a unified view of microbial pathogenesis. The lectures will integrate concepts from the disciplines of immunology, bacteriology, virology, and parasitology, to cover aspects of host cell biology and the innate and acquired immune responses of the host to infection. A range of medically relevant human and animal pathogens will be detailed, focusing on the host's response to commensalism versus disease, the mechanisms of host invasion, nutrient acquisition, and modulation of the host's immune response. The course will also discuss current disease interventions and the challenges facing antimicrobial therapy and vaccine development. This integrated and comparative view of the cellular and molecular interaction between infectious agents and the host's immune response will be a capstone of the student's exposure to infection biology.", "catalogBreadth": "", "catalogDistr": "(PBS-AS, BIO-AS)", "catalogEnrollmentPriority": null, "catalogLang": "", "catalogForbiddenOverlaps": "", "catalogAttribute": "", "catalogWhenOffered": "Spring (offered alternate years).", "catalogComments": "", "catalogPrereqCoreq": "Recommended prerequisite: BIOMS 4040/BIOMI 4040\u00a0or BIOMS 4090/BIOMI 4090\u00a0or BIOMS 4310/BIOMI 4310\u00a0or BIOMS 4150\u00a0or permission of instructor.", "catalogPrereq": null, "catalogCoreq": null, "catalogFee": "", "catalogSatisfiesReq": "", "catalogPermission": "", "catalogCourseSubfield": "", "catalogOutcomes": ["Identify and distinguish the approaches viruses, bacteria and parasites use to counteract or interact with components of the host immune response to allow their replication and spread.", "Integrate at a cellular and molecular level the host's response to prevent infections with the strategies infectious agents employ to overcome host."], "acadCareer": "UG", "acadGroup": "AG", "lastTermsOffered": null, "exploreCriteriaIds": null}, {"strm": 2783, "crseId": 362908, "crseOfferNbr": 1, "subject": "BIOMS", "catalogNbr": "4980", "titleShort": "Undergrad Supervised Teaching", "titleLong": "Undergraduate Supervised Teaching", "crseAttrs": [], "crseAttrValueGroups": [], "enrollGroups": [{"classSections": [{"ssrComponent": "IND", "ssrComponentLong": "Independent Study", "section": "601", "classNbr": 14918, "meetings": [{"classMtgNbr": 1, "timeStart": "", "timeEnd": "", "startDt": "02/08/2021", "endDt": "05/14/2021", "instructors": [], "pattern": "", "facilityDescr": null, "bldgDescr": null, "facilityDescrshort": null, "meetingTopicDescription": ""}], "notes": [{"classNotesSeq": 1, "descrlong": "Prerequisite: previous enrollment in course to be taught or equivalent.  Students must register using the special study form available online: https://dust.cals.cornell.edu/IndStudyPolicy.aspx.\nA&S students receive transcript credit only; cannot be used toward graduation"}], "campus": "MAIN", "campusDescr": "Ithaca", "location": "ITH", "locationDes